1
00:03:08,888 --> 00:03:14,888
Survival is her only option

2
00:06:17,777 --> 00:06:23,777
Frenzy is a horror, thriller, tv movie film directed by Jose Montesinos.

3
00:09:26,666 --> 00:09:32,666
A group of friends run a popular travel vlog that helps fund their adventures.

4
00:12:35,555 --> 00:12:41,555
The leader of the group brings along her younger sister for
the next scuba diving trip to an isolated cove.

5
00:15:44,444 --> 00:15:50,444
But when their plane crashes, the two sisters must use their strength,
resourcefulness and immense courage to survive a pack of great white sharks.

6
00:18:53,333 --> 00:18:59,333
Lindsey, played by Aubrey Reynolds.

7
00:22:02,222 --> 00:22:08,222
Paige, played by Gina Vitori.

8
00:25:11,111 --> 00:25:17,111
Kahaia, played by Lanett Tachel.

9
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:26,000
Seb, played by Taylor Jorgensen.

10
00:31:28,888 --> 00:31:34,888
Evan, played by Michael S. New.

11
00:34:37,777 --> 00:34:43,777
Island Pilot, played by Pope Bustos.

12
00:37:46,666 --> 00:37:52,666
Frenzy is a 1972 British neo-noir thriller film directed by Alfred
Hitchcock in the penultimate feature film of his extensive career.

13
00:40:55,555 --> 00:41:01,555
The screenplay by Anthony Shaffer is based on the 1966 novel Goodbye
Piccadilly, Farewell Leicester Square by Arthur La Bern.

14
00:44:04,444 --> 00:44:10,444
The film stars Jon Finch, Alec McCowen and Barry Foster and features Billie Whitelaw,
Anna Massey, Barbara Leigh-Hunt, Bernard Cribbins and Vivien Merchant.

15
00:47:13,333 --> 00:47:19,333
The original music score was composed by Ron Goodwin.

16
00:50:22,222 --> 00:50:28,222
The plot centres on a serial killer in contemporary London
and the ex-RAF serviceman he implicates.

17
00:53:31,111 --> 00:53:37,111
In a very early scene there is dialogue that mentions two actual London serial murder cases:
the Christie murders in the 1940s–1950s and the Jack the Ripper murders in 1888.

18
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:46,000
Frenzy was the third and final film that Hitchcock made in
Britain after he moved to Hollywood in 1939.

19
00:59:48,888 --> 00:59:54,888
The other two were Under Capricorn (1949) and Stage Fright (1950).

20
01:02:57,777 --> 01:03:03,777
The last film he made in Britain before his move to the
United States was Jamaica Inn (1939).

21
01:06:06,666 --> 01:06:12,666
Frenzy was the only Hitchcock film given an R rating in the
United States during its initial release.

22
01:09:15,555 --> 01:09:21,555
Frenzy was screened at the 1972 Cannes Film Festival, but it
was not entered into the main competition.

23
01:12:24,444 --> 01:12:30,444
Some critics—such as Raymond Foery, author of Alfred Hitchcock's Frenzy: The Last Masterpiece—consider Frenzy the last
great Hitchcock film, and a return to form after his two previous works: Torn Curtain (1966) and Topaz (1969).

24
01:15:33,333 --> 01:15:39,333
The film was a commercial success, grossing $12.6 million at the box office worldwide ($96.9
million with 2025 inflation) from a $2 million ($15.3 million in 2025 inflation) budget.

25
01:18:42,222 --> 01:18:48,222
The film explores themes of shark attack, shark,
survival horror, plane crash, sea plane.

26
01:21:51,111 --> 01:21:57,111
The tone is lighthearted and dark and intense.
